8\n- concepts/outlook-inbox-triage-2026-08-06\n- concepts/outlook-inbox-triage-2026-08-07\n- concepts/outlook-inbox-triage-action-list\n- concepts/pandas-nullable-int64-fillna-gotcha\n- concepts/perch-api-integration-reference\n- concepts/perch-weight-room-domain-feature\n- concepts/playwright-cli-install-mcp-disable\n- concepts/policy-document-humanization-diff-against-original\n- concepts/powerpoint-free-pptx-renderer\n- concepts/pre-destructive-git-backup-discipline\n- concepts/preregistration-as-substitute-for-external-review\n- concepts/printing-press-cli-slash-commands\n- concepts/project-docs-file-map-pattern\n- concepts/project-triage-retrospective\n- concepts/public-repo-privacy-audit-read-inside\n- concepts/quota-metered-pipeline-execution-strategy\n- concepts/risk-stratification-engine-availability-hazard\n- concepts/rounded-counts-in-context-documents\n- concepts/rsync-symlink-conversion-stale-directory\n- concepts/rtp-clinic-deliverable-finalization\n- concepts/rtp-presentation-tooling-round-trip\n- concepts/s2-evaluation-preregistered-analysis-design\n- concepts/s2-evaluation-v1-closeout-v2-scope\n- concepts/scratch-folder-artifact-durability\n- concepts/secondbrain-vault-infrastructure\n- concepts/session-context-footprint-audit\n- concepts/session-transcript-tracking-repo-growth\n- concepts/skill-description-budget-ceiling\n- concepts/skill-namespace-collisions\n- concepts/skills-repo-git-hygiene\n- concepts/skills-repo-worktree-branch-cleanup\n- concepts/skylar-zinnecker-cmj-rtc-reports\n- concepts/speedprofiling-gps-playbyplay-alignment\n- concepts/speedprofiling-keystoneos-vault-integration\n- concepts/speedprofiling-velocity-project\n- concepts/sports-coaching-slide-design-system\n- concepts/sportscience-research-repo-batch-import\n- concepts/stack-doctor-sweep-2026-08-11\n- concepts/stale-error-log-archival-discipline\n- concepts/stale-memory-router-points-at-wrong-answer\n- concepts/status-report-design-ported-back-to-skill-template\n- concepts/subagent-driven-development\n- concepts/submodule-conversion-breaks-rsync-backup\n- concepts/surgery-nlp-extraction\n- concepts/system-health-check-remediation\n- concepts/tezino-camp-prep-spc-monitoring\n- concepts/the-monitor-newsletter\n- concepts/tier-a-memory-as-the-durable-payload-of-a-verification-session\n- concepts/tmux-vscode-integrated-terminal-setup\n- concepts/unattended-cross-machine-push-collisions\n- concepts/vendored-copy-drift-within-a-repo\n- concepts/vendored-skill-hand-install-pattern\n- concepts/verify-the-diagnosis-in-a-received-report\n- concepts/visible-regeneration-over-silenced-steps\n- concepts/vscode-terminal-streaming-flicker\n- concepts/weekly-review-ritual-secondbrain\n- concepts/weekly-review-synthesis-workflow\n- concepts/windows-backup-recovery-inventory\n- concepts/windows-backup-task-trigger-gotchas\n- concepts/windows-git-ssh-https-rewrite\n- concepts/windows-setx-env-propagation\n- concepts/windows-task-scheduler-path-config\n- concepts/worktree-remote-merge-verification\n- concepts/zotero-api-integration-reference\n- concepts/zotero-research-library-pp-cli\n- connections/a-killed-process-leaves-debris-that-blocks-the-retry\n- connections/a-stale-pointer-resolves-cleanly-to-the-wrong-answer\n- connections/a-type-change-at-the-source-breaks-the-mirror\n- connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set\n- connections/always-loaded-context-is-the-expensive-place-to-be-wrong\n- connections/api-credential-assumptions-can-be-backwards\n- connections/athlete-monitoring-communication-grammar\n- connections/background-task-exit-code-false-failure\n- connections/bound-the-baseline-window-or-it-decides-the-verdict\n- connections/catapult-api-docs-and-tezino-token-issue\n- connections/catapult-friction-spans-auth-and-data-layers\n- connections/catapult-token-friction-across-baylor-projects\n- connections/change-the-checker-down-or-sideways\n- connections/check-version-control-before-mass-editing\n- connections/consult-the-governing-record-before-framing-the-output\n- connections/contaminated-baselines-distort-load-narratives\n- connections/document-the-mechanism-not-the-magic-number\n- connections/dont-baseline-on-the-injured-state\n- connections/durable-log-is-the-contract-for-scheduled-work\n- connections/every-layer-reported-healthy-except-the-destination\n- connections/fall-camp-planning-and-load-simulator\n- connections/fix-the-generator-not-the-artifacts\n- connections/fix-the-sentence-everywhere-it-was-copied\n- connections/fix-the-viewport-not-the-observer\n- connections/git-repository-boundaries-should-be-explicit\n- connections/git-scope-errors-inflate-the-work-estimate\n- connections/ground-truth-checks-must-match-the-lying-layer\n- connections/hindsight-leakage-and-pregame-freeze-capture\n- connections/hook-execution-environment-path-assumptions\n- connections/inbox-triage-and-morning-briefing-taxonomy\n- connections/install-time-capture-goes-stale-when-host-moves\n- connections/install-time-environment-capture-rots-silently\n- connections/instrumentation-that-normalizes-the-thing-under-test\n- connections/keep-data-retrievable-but-out-of-standing-context\n- connections/keep-it-retrievable-not-resident\n- connections/mandatory-routing-language-outranks-runtime-capability\n- connections/memory-compiler-tier-independence-under-failure\n- connections/mrsi-noise-vs-narrative-reliance\n- connections/multi-stage-review-catches-agent-blind-spots\n- connections/name-and-flag-are-not-the-artifact\n- connections/narrowest-sufficient-escalation-over-blanket-bypass\n- connections/newer-is-not-more-complete\n- connections/obsidian-vault-as-knowledge-hub\n- connections/preconditions-that-check-the-wrong-thing\n- connections/preserve-the-prior-version-before-restyling\n- connections/refuse-at-the-earliest-unmet-precondition\n- connections/refuse-upstream-or-verify-downstream\n- connections/report-the-gap-rather-than-the-fraction\n- connections/retrieval-score-vs-advisor-grounding\n- connections/reviewer-unavailability-as-a-forcing-function\n- connections/reviewers-cannot-audit-decision-order\n- connections/silent-omission-defeats-per-stage-success\n- connections/sports-tech-vendor-integration-friction\n- connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question\n- connections/step-down-to-ground-truth-when-the-surface-lies\n- connections/substitute-a-primitive-when-the-layer-is-unreliable\n- connections/test-the-layer-before-blaming-the-artifact\n- connections/the-default-is-the-real-decision\n- connections/the-execution-context-silently-edits-the-work-set\n- connections/the-monitor-is-the-defect\n- connections/the-preservation-step-must-itself-be-verified\n- connections/the-reference-window-decides-the-verdict\n- connections/unactioned-urgent-items-age-across-digests\n- connections/vald-forcedecks-cmj-data-across-projects\n- connections/vault-constraints-shape-tooling-design\n- connections/vendor-action-bypasses-os-setting\n- connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output\n- connections/web-scraping-skill-and-readme-doc-trick\n- connections/windows-bash-tool-no-output-powershell-fallback\n- connections/windows-defaults-outlive-the-windows-machine\n- connections/windows-process-env-fixed-at-launch\n\n## Candidate 1\n(they share 8 neighbours; lexical overlap 0.089; written 8 days apart)\n\n### A: concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Closing a 49-Folder Backup Manifest Coverage Gap\"\naliases: [49 unmanifested DEV folders, backup-manifest.json 78 to 125, job count 28 to 76, manifest coverage gap, AI_Analyst token-dashboard removed, DexaProject Google Drive warning, migration left folders unmanifested]\ntags: [backup, automation, macos, keystoneos, verification, maintenance, git]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-09.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-09\nupdated: 2026-09-09\n---\n\n# Closing a 49-Folder Backup Manifest Coverage Gap\n\nOn 2026-09-09 a KeystoneOS daily backup run returned `status: ERROR` with two problems: the `~/.claude` repo was one commit behind GitHub (blocking its push) and 49 folders under `~/DEV` were absent from `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json`. Rather than triage the 49, the user confirmed that all of them mattered — the gap was a residue of an earlier migration — and every one was added. The manifest grew from 78 to 125 entries and the backup's job count went from 28 to 76.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The gap was invisible.** Folders absent from the manifest are never backed up, and their absence produces no error that reads as urgent — only an advisory `manifest-unmanaged` class. The 49-folder hole had existed since a prior migration.\n- **All 49 were added, not triaged:** 47 as git repos (working-tree capture to the CRASHBOX SSD) and 2 as plain folders (`_ARCHIVE`, `docs` — SSD copy only, no repo semantics).\n- **Two stale entries were removed** (`AI_Analyst`, `token-dashboard`) after confirming both had already moved into `~/DEV/_ARCHIVE/`, leaving 125 entries. Manifest hygiene runs in both directions: adding new folders and retiring moved ones.\n- **`DexaProject`'s warning was kept deliberately** — it lives on Google Drive intentionally, so its manifest complaint is an accepted permanent condition rather than a defect to silence. A separate later finding in the same day established that the *GitHub* copy of `DexaProject` still holds a patient CSV and must be deleted.\n- **The `~/.claude` behind-by-one state was resolved with `git pull --rebase --autostash`,** preserving two uncommitted edits (`settings.json`, `hooks/herdr-agent-state.sh`). Backups never auto-merge by design.\n- **Commits were made but not pushed** — `~/.claude` at `dc54c97` and `~/.claude/global-memory` at `38e462b` — with the push deliberately left to the next scheduled backup run.\n\n## Details\n\nThis is the largest instance yet of the manifest-drift failure class first catalogued in [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]] (three folders) and re-observed at [[concepts/backup-error-log-recurring-failures-2026-08-16]] (five folders). The escalation from three to five to forty-nine is informative: the earlier gaps were ordinary drift from folders created faster than manifest entries, whereas this one had a single structural cause — a migration that moved or created many directories at once while the manifest, being hand-maintained, stayed where it was. Bulk moves are precisely the event a curated list cannot survive, which strengthens the standing proposal in [[concepts/dev-folder-manifest-default-deny]] to derive the manifest from a directory scan rather than maintaining it by hand. The default-deny guard did its job in that the folders were reported rather than silently excluded, but reporting alone is only sufficient when the report is acted on, and forty-nine entries accumulating across weeks shows the report being observed and deferred.\n\nThe removal of `AI_Analyst` and `token-dashboard` is the neglected half of manifest maintenance. A manifest entry pointing at a folder that has moved into `_ARCHIVE` will either fail loudly (noise) or resolve to a stale path (worse), and neither outcome is caught by any check that only asks \"is every folder covered?\" The `DexaProject` decision demonstrates the third state a manifest entry can occupy: an accepted, documented exception whose warning is expected output rather than a pending task. Recording the reason — it lives on Google Drive intentionally — is what prevents a future maintenance pass from either \"fixing\" it or being confused by it, the same discipline applied to the two large graph files kept deliberately in the 2026-09-01 classification pass. Notably, the same day's repo-visibility audit found that this deliberate local exception coexists with a genuine problem at a different layer: `erash11/DexaProject` is still a GitHub repository containing a patient CSV, awaiting deletion. A documented exception on one axis does not clear the artifact on every axis.\n\nThe `git pull --rebase --autostash` choice is worth isolating as policy. The backup job pushes several repos, and a repo that is behind its remote cannot push; the tempting resolutions are a force-push (destroys remote history) or a merge (creates a commit the operator did not author). Rebasing with autostash preserves both the remote's history and the two uncommitted working-tree edits, and the stated principle — backups never auto-merge — keeps the backup system from making content decisions on the operator's behalf. This is the same restraint shown when a two-writer SecondBrain conflict was resolved by staging both sides rather than adjudicating it, per [[concepts/stack-doctor-sweep-2026-08-11]]. The decision to commit `~/.claude` and `global-memory` without pushing follows the same logic in the other direction: the push is the backup's job, so leaving it to the next scheduled run keeps one owner for that action rather than racing it.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/dev-folder-manifest-default-deny]] — The fail-closed guard that surfaced the 49 folders; this run is the strongest argument yet for its auto-derivation follow-up.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]] — The original three-folder instance of the same failure class.\n- [[concepts/backup-error-log-recurring-failures-2026-08-16]] — The five-folder `manifest-unmanaged` instance between that one and this.\n- [[concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count]] — The completeness metric this session reset from 28 to 76.\n- [[concepts/on-demand-backup-skill-and-ssd-completeness]] — The job-count completeness tell, which this session extends and complicates by moving the baseline.\n- [[concepts/crashbox-ssd-backup-baseline]] — The SSD leg that now receives working-tree captures for 47 additional repos.\n- [[concepts/public-repo-privacy-audit-read-inside]] — The same day's finding that `DexaProject` is also a live public GitHub repo holding a patient CSV.\n- [[concepts/stack-doctor-sweep-2026-08-11]] — The precedent for resolving repo divergence without adjudicating content on the operator's behalf.\n- [[connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set]] — The general shape: a clean run over a work set that was never complete.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-09.md]] — Backup repair session (19:17): initial run `status: ERROR` with `~/.claude` one commit behind and 49 unmanifested `~/DEV` folders; user confirmed all 49 mattered (\"we did a migration earlier\"); 47 added as git repos and 2 as plain folders (`_ARCHIVE`, `docs`); job count 28 → 76 and manifest 78 → 125 entries; `AI_Analyst` and `token-dashboard` removed after confirming both had moved to `~/DEV/_ARCHIVE/`; `DexaProject` warning kept as an intentional Google Drive exception; `~/.claude` fixed with `git pull --rebase --autostash` preserving two uncommitted edits; commits `dc54c97` and `38e462b` left unpushed for the next backup run.\n\n```\n\n### B: connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Connection: A Status Field Answers a Narrower Question Than the One Being Asked\"\nconnects:\n  - \"concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag\"\n  - \"concepts/background-monitor-signal-reliability\"\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-08-31.md\"\ncreated: 2026-08-31\nupdated: 2026-09-01\n---\n\n# Connection: A Status Field Answers a Narrower Question Than the One Being Asked\n\n## The Connection\n\n`launchctl list` column 2 reports the exit code of the *last scheduled* invocation of a job. The question being asked of it is \"is this backup healthy?\" These are different questions, and on 2026-08-31 they diverged: the manifest was fixed at 10:31, the manual re-run exited clean, and the status field stayed red because no scheduled run had occurred since. The same divergence appears in the 2026-08-06 background-monitor findings, where a comment-count bump answered \"did the count change?\" while being read as \"is there a new finding?\" — and counted the agent's own replies toward the total.\n\n## Key Insight\n\nThese are not cases of a signal being *wrong*. Each field reports accurately on its own narrow predicate; the failure is that the predicate is narrower than the operational question, and nothing at the display layer marks the gap. `launchctl` never claimed to know about out-of-band runs. A comment counter never claimed to distinguish authorship. The reader supplies the broader interpretation, and it holds right up until an event occurs that the field's scope excludes — a manual re-run, a self-authored reply, a duplicate delivery during a lockout.\n\nThis yields a specific diagnostic move, distinct from the general \"verify at ground truth\" rule: before reconciling a suspicious signal against an artifact, first ask what proposition the signal actually asserts. Doing so often dissolves the anomaly without any investigation — the 2026-08-31 red status needed no debugging once its scope was named, and the correct action became *leave it alone*, because the field will self-correct at the next scheduled tick. Forcing a manual re-run to turn it green would have been cosmetic, treating the display as the state. Where the earlier lessons say \"check the artifact when the layer lies,\" this one says \"check the layer's scope before deciding it lied at all.\"\n\n## Evidence\n\n- 2026-08-31: \"`launchctl list` column 2 reflects the *last scheduled* run, not manual/out-of-band runs — a green backup log can coexist with a red launchd status. Confirm against the backup log timeline before treating the exit code as a live failure.\" The recommendation was explicitly to leave the stale code alone since it self-clears at the next 09:00 run.\n- 2026-08-06: background monitor comment-count bumps counted the agent's own replies as new findings — the counter answered \"count changed\" and was read as \"new external finding.\"\n- 2026-08-06: monitor events were silently dropped during a session-limit lockout (~15 duplicates, no dedup, backoff, or alert) — delivery status reported on transmission, not on receipt.\n- 2026-07-18: the SpeedProfiling background task's \"exit code 1\" reported on the process-management wrapper, not on whether the R script's work completed — the log showed a clean end-to-end run.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]] — The 2026-08-31 instance: a scheduled-run-scoped exit code read as current health.\n- [[concepts/background-monitor-signal-reliability]] — Comment-count and delivery signals whose scope excluded self-authorship and lockout drops.\n- [[connections/background-task-exit-code-false-failure]] — The neighbouring rule for when a signal genuinely misreports: reconcile against on-disk artifacts.\n- [[connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output]] — The parent discipline; this connection refines it by inserting a scope check before the verification step.\n\n```\n## Candidate 2\n(they share 4 neighbours; lexical overlap 0.042; written 138 days apart)\n\n### A: concepts/vendored-skill-hand-install-pattern\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Vendored Skill Hand-Install Pattern (~/.agents/skills → Claude + Codex)\"\naliases: [hand-install skills, davidondrej skills repo, npx skills add blocked, skill symlink shape, .skill-lock.json missing hand-installed, persistent-localhost, anti-sleep, 58 skills vetted, 151 to 156 skills]\ntags: [claude-code, skills, installation, macos, symlinks, tooling, workflow]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-04.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-04\nupdated: 2026-09-07\n---\n\n# Vendored Skill Hand-Install Pattern (~/.agents/skills → Claude + Codex)\n\nOn 2026-09-04, the `davidondrej/skills` GitHub repository (58 skills) was vetted against the existing environment and five skills were installed by hand after the official `npx skills add` installer was blocked by the auto-mode permission classifier. The session established a working install shape — vendor directory plus two layers of symlink — verified it empirically rather than by assumption, and documented a real cost of the manual route: hand-installed skills are invisible to the upstream lockfile and therefore to `skills update`.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **53 of 58 skills rejected, with reasons by category:** duplicates of already-installed capability (superpowers, session-handoff, CreateSkill, codex plugin, launchd), dependence on tools absent from this machine (bb, cmux, Herdr, Pi, Cursor, Postgres), a paid DeepAPI key requirement (`risky-changes` and all ten `research-and-web` skills), and hardcoding of the author's own name (`file-tree`, `stop-overthinking`, `short`, `remind`).\n- **Five installed, taking the count 151 → 156:** `persistent-localhost` (runs dev servers as launchd jobs, replacing `&`/`nohup`), `anti-sleep` (`caffeinate` for long or overnight runs), and three slash-only skills — `/decisions`, `/before-building`, `/next-decision`.\n- **`global-agent-guardrails` was skipped for a concrete conflict,** not redundancy alone: `dcg` already covers the ground, and the skill's denylist blocks `security find-generic-password`, which the Fireflies key read depends on.\n- **The working install shape:** copy `SKILL.md` plus `scripts/` and `agents/` into `~/.agents/skills/<name>/`, `chmod +x` the scripts, symlink *relatively* into `~/.claude/skills/<name>`, then symlink `~/.codex/skills/<name>` at `~/.claude/skills/<name>` — every Codex entry points at the Claude path, not at `~/.agents/skills` directly.\n- **Hand-installed skills are absent from `~/.agents/.skill-lock.json`,** so `skills update` will not see them. Registering them retroactively is possible by running the official installer behind a `!` prefix to bypass the classifier; it may simply report \"already installed.\"\n- **Both installs were tested, not assumed:** `persistent-localhost` served a real HTTP 200 on port 5199 and stopped cleanly; `anti-sleep` reached `ASSERTIONS=active` and stopped leaving no stray plists.\n\n## Details\n\nThe vetting ratio — 5 accepted out of 58 — is the substantive finding, and the four rejection categories are reusable as a triage rubric for any third-party skill bundle. Duplication is the largest bucket and the cheapest to check, since an existing capability makes the new skill pure listing overhead against the description budget documented in [[concepts/skill-description-budget-ceiling]]. Missing-dependency and paid-key rejections are hard constraints discoverable by reading the skill body. The author-name-hardcoding category is the least obvious and the most instructive: a skill written for personal use may embed its author's identity in prompts or paths, which makes it silently wrong rather than broken when installed elsewhere. The `global-agent-guardrails` rejection is a fifth, sharper category — active interference, where the skill's denylist would have broken an unrelated working integration (the Fireflies keychain read). That is a case where \"we already have coverage\" understates the reason to decline.\n\nThe symlink topology is worth stating precisely because it is not the obvious arrangement. The canonical files live once in `~/.agents/skills/<name>/`; `~/.claude/skills/<name>` is a *relative* symlink into that directory; and `~/.codex/skills/<name>` symlinks to the Claude path rather than to the vendor path. Routing Codex through Claude rather than pointing both harnesses at the vendor directory means the Claude path is the single indirection point — moving or replacing the underlying skill requires updating one link, not two. The insistence on a relative link for the Claude leg matters given this environment's history with committed symlinks: absolute links that encode a home directory are exactly what broke during the Windows→Mac migration described in [[concepts/committed-symlink-repo-landmine]].\n\nThe lockfile gap is the genuine cost of hand-installing and should be recorded wherever the install is documented. `~/.agents/.skill-lock.json` is the manifest `skills update` consults, so a skill installed outside the installer is functionally present but permanently unmanaged — it will never receive an upstream fix. This is the same silent-omission shape as a backup manifest that does not list a folder: every managed item reports success, and the unmanaged item is invisible precisely because nothing knows to look for it. The offered remedy (re-running the official installer behind `!` purely to register the entries) resolves the bookkeeping without repeating the file operations. A final procedural note from the same session: two prior claims recorded in `project_vendored-skill-install.md` were disproven during this work and corrected in Tier A memory, which is the correct disposition for memory that testing contradicts.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/dcg-guard-false-positives-native-tool-workaround]] — The `rm -rf` guard rule that forced the install script into a copy-only shape, and the `>`-in-Python false positive hit during the same session.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-permission-modes]] — The auto-mode classifier that blocked `npx skills add`, and the `!` one-off bypass offered as the lockfile-registration route.\n- [[concepts/skill-description-budget-ceiling]] — Why installing marginal skills is not free: every listed skill consumes the session description budget, making the 53 rejections a direct saving.\n- [[concepts/committed-symlink-repo-landmine]] — Prior symlink trouble in this same skills tree; motivates the relative-link discipline used here.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-meta-skills-bundle]] — The earlier precedent of cherry-picking a subset from a multi-skill bundle rather than accepting an all-or-nothing install.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] — Tier A is where the two disproven install claims were corrected.\n- [[connections/silent-omission-defeats-per-stage-success]] — The lockfile gap is an instance: every installed skill works, yet the update path cannot see them.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-04.md]] — Skill review and install session (13:18): all 58 `davidondrej/skills` vetted with four rejection categories, five installed (151 → 156), `global-agent-guardrails` skipped for its `security find-generic-password` denylist conflict, `npx skills add` blocked by the auto-mode classifier, the `~/.agents/skills` → `~/.claude/skills` → `~/.codex/skills` symlink shape, the `dcg` `rm -rf` rule forcing a copy-only script, `.skill-lock.json` invisibility with the `!`-prefixed registration remedy, empirical tests of `persistent-localhost` (HTTP 200 on port 5199) and `anti-sleep` (`ASSERTIONS=active`), and two disproven claims corrected in `project_vendored-skill-install.md`.\n\n```\n\n### B: connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Connection: Verify Directly, Don't Trust the Intermediate Layer\"\nconnects:\n  - \"concepts/sportscience-research-repo-batch-import\"\n  - \"concepts/the-monitor-newsletter\"\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-04-22.md\"\ncreated: 2026-04-22\nupdated: 2026-04-22\n---\n\n# Connection: Verify Directly, Don't Trust the Intermediate Layer\n\n## The Connection\n\nBoth substantive sessions on 2026-04-22 ran into a version of the same problem: a tool or agent produced output that *looked* fine - a citation with a plausible author list, a photo wired into HTML with a correct relative path - but was actually wrong or unverifiable through the usual checking method. In [[concepts/sportscience-research-repo-batch-import]], a background repair agent's citation fixes for IDs 286, 287, and 358 contained hallucinated author lists despite correctly identifying the papers' topics. In [[concepts/the-monitor-newsletter]], Blake Wright's photo failed to appear in the localhost preview server even though the HTML and file path were both correct.\n\n## Key Insight\n\nIn both cases, the fix was not to redo the original work but to change the *verification method* to something closer to the ground truth. The hallucinated citations were caught by extracting the actual PDF text with PyPDF2 and reading it directly, rather than trusting the agent's summarized output. The \"broken\" photo was confirmed to be fine by opening the HTML file directly in Chrome via `file://`, rather than trusting the brainstorm preview server's rendering. The shared lesson: when an agent, tool, or service produces output that looks plausible but can't be easily double-checked through the same channel, step down to a more primitive, lower-level check (raw file text, direct file rendering) before accepting the result as correct.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- 2026-04-22 (10:57): \"Background agent produced `docs/batch_r7_fixes.json` but hallucinated wrong author lists for IDs 286, 287, and 358... Used PyPDF2 text extraction to verify actual authors and corrected all three citations before committing.\"\n- 2026-04-22 (17:21): \"Photo failed to render in localhost preview — identified as a server limitation (brainstorm preview server doesn't serve image files), not an HTML error... always verify photo rendering by opening the HTML directly in Chrome via file:// URL.\"\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/sportscience-research-repo-batch-import]]\n- [[concepts/the-monitor-newsletter]]\n\n```\n## Candidate 3\n(they share 4 neighbours; lexical overlap 0.046; written 140 days apart)\n\n### A: concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Cross-Machine Conflict File Triage: 36 Files Collapse to 4\"\naliases: [Windows Mac conflict files, 26 CRLF-only conflicts, graphify-out portfolio.json Mac newer, weekly_forecast_log.csv 324 rows, mac wins dangerous for append-only logs, newer is not more complete, SoftwareTech DRAFT 1.0 MB vs 0.4 MB]\ntags: [git, sync, windows, macos, data-integrity, verification, gotcha]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-09.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-09\nupdated: 2026-09-09\n---\n\n# Cross-Machine Conflict File Triage: 36 Files Collapse to 4\n\nThirty-six files flagged as conflicting between a Windows machine and a Mac were diffed individually on 2026-09-09 and reduced to four genuine conflicts. Twenty-six differed only by CRLF line endings and six were `graphify-out/` or `portfolio.json` artifacts where the Mac copy is simply newer. The residue produced the session's most consequential finding: a blanket \"Mac wins\" resolution rule silently discards data when the file is an append-only log.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **26 of 36 conflicts were CRLF-only** — a Windows line-ending artifact rather than a content difference, and safe to delete without inspection once identified.\n- **6 more were `graphify-out/` and `portfolio.json` generated files** where the Mac copy is newer and the Windows copy has no independent value.\n- **The 32 safe files can be deleted with zero risk;** only four require human comparison.\n- **\"Mac wins\" is wrong for append-only logs.** `CFB_Victory_Model/data/weekly_forecast_log.csv` is *smaller* on the Mac; the Windows copy holds 324 forecast rows dating back to 2026-08-26 that exist nowhere else. Newer does not mean more complete.\n- **Three `SoftwareTech_Mgmt_Framework/deliverables/` Word/Excalidraw files remain open**, with the DRAFT at 1.0 MB on Windows against 0.4 MB on the Mac — a size gap large enough that neither timestamp nor size alone should decide it.\n\n## Details\n\nThe 89% collapse rate is itself the first lesson: a conflict *count* produced by a sync tool measures byte-level divergence, which is a far weaker property than semantic divergence. CRLF normalization has repeatedly made this stack's working trees lie toward difference, and the same mechanism inflates any cross-machine conflict list generated on a repository that has passed through Windows. Acting on the raw count of 36 would have meant 36 manual comparisons; diffing first cut the human work by an order of magnitude and, more importantly, concentrated attention on the four files where judgment was actually required. The generalizable move is to classify the conflict list by *kind of difference* before resolving any individual entry.\n\nThe forecast-log finding is the durable one and it inverts an intuition that most sync tooling encodes as a default. Last-writer-wins is a reasonable rule for a document, where the newer version is a revision of the older and contains it. It is actively destructive for an append-only log, where the two copies are not revisions of one another but divergent branches of an accumulating record — each machine appended its own rows, and the correct resolution is a *merge*, not a selection. That the Mac copy was smaller is the tell that should trigger the check, and it is a tell that a timestamp-based rule cannot see by construction. The 324 Windows-only rows back to 2026-08-26 are exactly the kind of data whose loss would be discovered months later, during an evaluation, as an unexplained gap — the same class of silent, delayed damage as the `.errors.log` archive overwrite from earlier the same day, and recoverable there only because git happened to hold a second copy.\n\nThe three unresolved `SoftwareTech_Mgmt_Framework` deliverables illustrate the boundary of automated triage. Word and Excalidraw files are opaque binaries whose size difference could mean embedded images, revision history, or genuinely more content, and no diff at the byte level distinguishes those. Deferring them to a hand comparison — rather than applying either machine-wins rule — is the correct call, and the same restraint applied when the two-writer SecondBrain briefing conflict was resolved by staging both sides rather than adjudicating on the spot.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/claude-code-worktree-gotchas]] — CRLF normalization as the mechanism inflating the conflict count.\n- [[connections/windows-defaults-outlive-the-windows-machine]] — CRLF, backslash paths, and `core.symlinks=false` persisting past the Windows machine; this is a further instance.\n- [[concepts/error-log-archive-collision-overwrite]] — The same-day sibling: a careless resolution destroying append-only history, recovered from git by luck.\n- [[concepts/dev-thin-repo-github-consolidation]] — The consolidation effort that surfaced the conflict list.\n- [[concepts/cfb-victory-model-evaluation]] — The project whose forecast log holds the 324 at-risk rows; the pregame freeze captures are the file's entire value.\n- [[connections/ground-truth-checks-must-match-the-lying-layer]] — Identifying the transforming layer (CRLF) before deciding what the difference means.\n- [[connections/step-down-to-ground-truth-when-the-surface-lies]] — A conflict flag is a surface signal; the diff is the ground truth beneath it.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-09.md]] — GitHub consolidation session (22:06): 36 flagged Windows/Mac conflict files diffed down to 4 real ones — 26 CRLF-only, 6 `graphify-out/`/`portfolio.json` where Mac is newer — with the 32 safe files queued for deletion; `CFB_Victory_Model/data/weekly_forecast_log.csv` identified as an append-only log where the Mac-wins rule kept the smaller file and 324 Windows-only rows back to 2026-08-26 exist nowhere else; merge offered but not yet performed; three `SoftwareTech_Mgmt_Framework/deliverables/` Word/Excalidraw files left for hand comparison with the DRAFT at 1.0 MB on Windows vs 0.4 MB on Mac.\n\n```\n\n### B: connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Connection: Verify Directly, Don't Trust the Intermediate Layer\"\nconnects:\n  - \"concepts/sportscience-research-repo-batch-import\"\n  - \"concepts/the-monitor-newsletter\"\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-04-22.md\"\ncreated: 2026-04-22\nupdated: 2026-04-22\n---\n\n# Connection: Verify Directly, Don't Trust the Intermediate Layer\n\n## The Connection\n\nBoth substantive sessions on 2026-04-22 ran into a version of the same problem: a tool or agent produced output that *looked* fine - a citation with a plausible author list, a photo wired into HTML with a correct relative path - but was actually wrong or unverifiable through the usual checking method. In [[concepts/sportscience-research-repo-batch-import]], a background repair agent's citation fixes for IDs 286, 287, and 358 contained hallucinated author lists despite correctly identifying the papers' topics. In [[concepts/the-monitor-newsletter]], Blake Wright's photo failed to appear in the localhost preview server even though the HTML and file path were both correct.\n\n## Key Insight\n\nIn both cases, the fix was not to redo the original work but to change the *verification method* to something closer to the ground truth. The hallucinated citations were caught by extracting the actual PDF text with PyPDF2 and reading it directly, rather than trusting the agent's summarized output. The \"broken\" photo was confirmed to be fine by opening the HTML file directly in Chrome via `file://`, rather than trusting the brainstorm preview server's rendering. The shared lesson: when an agent, tool, or service produces output that looks plausible but can't be easily double-checked through the same channel, step down to a more primitive, lower-level check (raw file text, direct file rendering) before accepting the result as correct.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- 2026-04-22 (10:57): \"Background agent produced `docs/batch_r7_fixes.json` but hallucinated wrong author lists for IDs 286, 287, and 358... Used PyPDF2 text extraction to verify actual authors and corrected all three citations before committing.\"\n- 2026-04-22 (17:21): \"Photo failed to render in localhost preview — identified as a server limitation (brainstorm preview server doesn't serve image files), not an HTML error... always verify photo rendering by opening the HTML directly in Chrome via file:// URL.\"\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/sportscience-research-repo-batch-import]]\n- [[concepts/the-monitor-newsletter]]\n\n```\n## Candidate 4\n(they share 4 neighbours; lexical overlap 0.05; written 138 days apart)\n\n### A: concepts/on-demand-backup-skill-and-ssd-completeness\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"The /backup Skill: Job Count as the Completeness Signal\"\naliases: [/backup skill, CRASHBOX not mounted, 9 jobs vs 31 jobs, CAPTURE and STATE-SSD job classes, diskutil list external empty, charge-only USB-C cable, run-result file not exit code, errors.log archived-2026-09-04, skill loads immediately no restart]\ntags: [backup, automation, macos, skills, hardware, verification, maintenance]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-04.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-09-07.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-04\nupdated: 2026-09-07\n---\n\n# The /backup Skill: Job Count as the Completeness Signal\n\nOn 2026-09-04 at 16:17, a partially-failed scheduled backup was re-run manually and the experience was turned into a reusable `/backup` skill. The session's central finding is a measurement lesson: a backup run reporting zero errors can still be silently incomplete when a target volume is unmounted, so the number of jobs completed — 9 without the CRASHBOX SSD attached versus 31 with it — is a better health signal than the error count. A secondary thread diagnosed the SSD's non-appearance as a charge-only USB-C cable rather than any software fault. On 2026-09-07 the tell was written into the skill file itself, with the mechanism identified and verified against real logs.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The scheduled job was fine; the network was not.** `com.keystone.dailybackup` fired on time at 09:05. Two repo pushes (`global-memory`, `skills`) failed from a transient network/DNS blip, and a manual re-run cleared them — the `.errors.log` entry described a symptom, not a broken scheduler.\n- **Zero errors ≠ complete.** With CRASHBOX unmounted, committed work still reached GitHub and Google Drive, but uncommitted, non-repo, and gitignored files were saved *nowhere*. The run reported no errors because every job it knew to attempt succeeded.\n- **Job count is the completeness metric:** 9 jobs OK without the SSD, 31 jobs OK with it. The gap is the entire local-only-file leg of the backup.\n- **The mechanism behind the tell (recorded 2026-09-07):** the `CAPTURE` and `STATE-SSD` job classes only exist when the drive is mounted, so their absence is what shrinks the count. Verified against two real runs — a 13:25 run without the SSD produced 4 repo pushes plus 6 Drive snaps (10 jobs), and a 13:46 run with it produced the same 10 plus 20 `CAPTURE` and 9 `STATE-SSD` jobs. This tell is now documented in `~/.claude/skills/backup/SKILL.md` rather than living only in the operator's head.\n- **The skill checks the run-result file, not exit codes,** reports skipped targets explicitly, and clears the stale `.errors.log` entry so session-start stops nagging about a resolved condition.\n- **A USB-C drive that powers on but never mounts is almost always a charge-only cable.** `diskutil list external` printing nothing at all — no external disk visible whatsoever — is the fast discriminator between a cable/port problem and a filesystem problem. Swapping the cable fixed it.\n- **A newly written skill file loads immediately** — no session restart is needed before invoking it.\n- Naming followed the nearest sibling: lowercase-kebab `backup` to match `claude-stack-doctor`, since no `_ALLCAPS` private skills exist in this workspace.\n\n## Details\n\nThe unmounted-target failure is the most transferable finding, and it is a specific instance of a status field answering a narrower question than the operator's. \"Did the backup succeed?\" was answered as \"did every attempted job succeed?\", which is true and useless when the set of attempted jobs is silently smaller than the set of required jobs. The nine-versus-thirty-one gap is not a partial degradation of coverage — it is precisely the class of data that has no other copy anywhere. Committed repository content is redundantly protected by GitHub and Google Drive, so the SSD leg exists specifically to capture uncommitted work, non-repo files, and gitignored artifacts. Losing exactly that leg while reporting a clean run is the worst possible arrangement of signal and reality, and it is why the new skill reports skipped targets explicitly rather than folding them into an aggregate verdict. This is the same defect shape as the backup manifest omission documented in [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]]: in both cases coverage was wrong while execution was perfect, and in both cases the aggregate success indicator was structurally incapable of noticing.\n\nThe 2026-09-07 follow-up hardened the tell from a remembered number into a documented, mechanism-backed check. Knowing that \"31 means good, 9 means bad\" is fragile — the totals shift as repos and Drive targets are added — whereas knowing that the `CAPTURE` and `STATE-SSD` job classes are conditional on the mount makes the check robust to changes in the other job counts. Writing it into `SKILL.md` also moves the knowledge to where the next operator (or the next session) will encounter it, rather than requiring recall. The verification method matters too: rather than reasoning from the manifest about what *should* run, the claim was checked against two real log entries taken minutes apart under both conditions, which is a natural A/B that isolates exactly the SSD-dependent jobs.\n\nThe hardware diagnosis contributes a small, reusable triage rule. When an external drive shows power indicators but never mounts, the instinct is to suspect the filesystem, the mount daemon, or permissions. `diskutil list external` returning *nothing* rules all of those out at once: a drive with a corrupt filesystem still enumerates as a device, so total absence from the device list places the fault below the software layer entirely — at the cable, the port, or the hub. USB-C makes this especially common because charge-only cables are physically identical to data-capable ones. The prescribed order — swap to a known data cable first, then change port or connect directly rather than through a hub — moves from cheapest to most disruptive, and the cable swap resolved it here.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]] — The closest prior instance: coverage failure with clean execution, and a status field scoped more narrowly than the operational question.\n- [[concepts/crashbox-ssd-backup-baseline]] — The first full baseline of this same SSD leg; documents the rsync-incremental behavior that makes re-running after a missed run cheap.\n- [[concepts/git-sync-status-versus-uncommitted-work]] — Why the SSD leg is the only protection during a deferred-commit window, when `ahead=0` reads green over an uncommitted working tree.\n- [[concepts/stale-error-log-archival-discipline]] — The archive-then-clear discipline the skill automates for `.errors.log`.\n- [[concepts/automation-stack-doctor]] — The sibling health-check skill whose naming convention `/backup` followed, and which surfaces backup freshness as a standing check.\n- [[concepts/offline-capable-pipeline-legs]] — The per-leg network-dependency framing; the transient push failure and the unmounted SSD are failures in two different legs with different remedies.\n- [[connections/background-task-exit-code-false-failure]] — The general rule the skill implements: reconcile against a durable artifact rather than trusting an exit code.\n- [[connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question]] — The \"0 errors\" verdict answering a narrower predicate than \"is the backup complete?\".\n- [[connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set]] — The shared shape across backup jobs, manifest folders, and lockfile-invisible skills.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-04.md]] — Manual backup and `/backup` skill session (16:17): `com.keystone.dailybackup` confirmed to have fired on time at 09:05 with two transient push failures cleared by a re-run, CRASHBOX unattached leaving uncommitted/non-repo/gitignored files unsaved, 9-versus-31 job count as the completeness signal, `diskutil list external` empty diagnosing a charge-only cable, lowercase-kebab naming after `claude-stack-doctor`, skill reading the run-result file and reporting skipped targets, stale `.errors.log` cleared with archive at `.errors.log.archived-2026-09-04`, immediate skill load without restart, and the final receipt `run-20260904-134638.txt` (7,801 files, 0 errors, 31 jobs OK).\n- [[daily/2026-09-07.md]] — Config-hygiene session (18:05): the job-count tell written into `~/.claude/skills/backup/SKILL.md`, mechanism identified as the `CAPTURE` and `STATE-SSD` job classes existing only when the drive is mounted, verified against a 13:25 run without the SSD (4 repo pushes + 6 Drive snaps) versus a 13:46 run with it (the same 10 plus 20 `CAPTURE` + 9 `STATE-SSD`), a stale `SKILL.md.bak-2026-08-29` deleted from the stack-doctor skill, and a `/claude-stack-doctor` run confirming backup OK with 31 targets.\n\n```\n\n### B: connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Connection: Verify Directly, Don't Trust the Intermediate Layer\"\nconnects:\n  - \"concepts/sportscience-research-repo-batch-import\"\n  - \"concepts/the-monitor-newsletter\"\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-04-22.md\"\ncreated: 2026-04-22\nupdated: 2026-04-22\n---\n\n# Connection: Verify Directly, Don't Trust the Intermediate Layer\n\n## The Connection\n\nBoth substantive sessions on 2026-04-22 ran into a version of the same problem: a tool or agent produced output that *looked* fine - a citation with a plausible author list, a photo wired into HTML with a correct relative path - but was actually wrong or unverifiable through the usual checking method. In [[concepts/sportscience-research-repo-batch-import]], a background repair agent's citation fixes for IDs 286, 287, and 358 contained hallucinated author lists despite correctly identifying the papers' topics. In [[concepts/the-monitor-newsletter]], Blake Wright's photo failed to appear in the localhost preview server even though the HTML and file path were both correct.\n\n## Key Insight\n\nIn both cases, the fix was not to redo the original work but to change the *verification method* to something closer to the ground truth. The hallucinated citations were caught by extracting the actual PDF text with PyPDF2 and reading it directly, rather than trusting the agent's summarized output. The \"broken\" photo was confirmed to be fine by opening the HTML file directly in Chrome via `file://`, rather than trusting the brainstorm preview server's rendering. The shared lesson: when an agent, tool, or service produces output that looks plausible but can't be easily double-checked through the same channel, step down to a more primitive, lower-level check (raw file text, direct file rendering) before accepting the result as correct.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- 2026-04-22 (10:57): \"Background agent produced `docs/batch_r7_fixes.json` but hallucinated wrong author lists for IDs 286, 287, and 358... Used PyPDF2 text extraction to verify actual authors and corrected all three citations before committing.\"\n- 2026-04-22 (17:21): \"Photo failed to render in localhost preview — identified as a server limitation (brainstorm preview server doesn't serve image files), not an HTML error... always verify photo rendering by opening the HTML directly in Chrome via file:// URL.\"\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/sportscience-research-repo-batch-import]]\n- [[concepts/the-monitor-newsletter]]\n\n```\n\n## Output format\n\nFor EVERY candidate, in order, emit exactly one verdict block:\n\n===VERDICT===\npair: <A path> | <B path>\nreal: yes\nreason: <one sentence>\n===END VERDICT===\n\nFor each verdict where real is yes, ALSO emit a connection article:\n\n===ARTICLE connections/<slug>.md===\n<full markdown: YAML frontmatter (title, tags, sources, created, updated) then\nbody with Key Points (3-5 bullets), Details (2+ paragraphs), and Related\nConcepts linking BOTH source articles plus at least one other>\n===END ARTICLE===\n\nSlugs are lowercase and hyphenated and should name the relationship, not the\ntwo topics. After all articles, emit ONE index block containing one row per\narticle created:\n\n===INDEX===\n| [[connections/<slug>]] | one-line summary | adjudicated | 2026-09-20 |\n===END INDEX===\n\nEmit ONLY these blocks - no preamble, no commentary.\n"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"dequeue","timestamp":"2026-09-20T07:32:30.534Z","sessionId":"842f520e-0b0f-4b3d-9120-1d366a04d2cb"}
{"parentUuid":null,"isSidechain":false,"promptId":"9ddb0f6e-ea34-4281-a3cd-a6ad336c20cf","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"You are judging candidate connections in a personal knowledge base.\n\nEach candidate below is a pair of articles that are NOT currently linked but\nshare neighbours in the wikilink graph. Structural proximity is only a hint -\nyour job is to decide whether a real, non-obvious relationship exists.\n\nREJECT when:\n- the two articles are merely about the same topic or domain\n- the relationship is obvious from the titles alone\n- a connection article would only restate what either article already says\n- the shared neighbours are generic infrastructure both happen to touch\n\nACCEPT only when a reader who already knew BOTH articles would still learn\nsomething from the relationship being made explicit - a shared root cause, a\ntransferable technique, a tension between two approaches, a pattern that\nrecurs across different domains.\n\nBe strict. Rejecting most candidates is the expected and correct outcome.\n\n## Articles you may [[wikilink]]\n\n- concepts/accidental-git-init-home-directory\n- concepts/advisor-invocation-failure-modes\n- concepts/applied-performance-biweekly-status-report\n- concepts/applied-performance-okr-system\n- concepts/arguable-threshold-design\n- concepts/at-utilization-benchmarks-project\n- concepts/ath-medicine-project\n- concepts/athlete-monitoring-rtp-presentation\n- concepts/athletics-performance-landing-page-spec\n- concepts/automated-layout-qa-gate\n- concepts/automation-stack-doctor\n- concepts/background-monitor-signal-reliability\n- concepts/backup-error-log-recurring-failures-2026-08-16\n- concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09\n- concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag\n- concepts/backup-manifest-symlink-false-alarm\n- concepts/backup-oom-kill-during-first-time-index\n- concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count\n- concepts/backup-verification-by-restore\n- concepts/bayl